  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nToday marked the final day of above-average temperatures as an\napproaching trough sweeps a cold front into the area overnight.\nThe cold front is currently draped through lower Michigan and\ninto eastern Indiana, with a narrow band of rain accompanying \nthe boundary. Rain and the front are expected to reach eastern \nOhio between 3-5AM Monday before progressing into western \nPennsylvania during the Monday morning commute. The probability \nof measurable precipitation decrease between 10am and noon as \nmid-level dry air advances into the region from the west. \n\nAs winds shift to the northwest, colder air moving across the\nrelatively warm Great Lakes will support the development of\nlake-enhanced showers Monday into early Tuesday. Some rain-snow\nmix will be possible north of I-80 between 1-7AM Tuesday.\nHowever, the potential for winter weather impacts remains very\nlow, with only a light dusting possible on the ground in isolated\nlocations. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nConfidence remains high that dry and quiet weather is expected\nTuesday afternoon into Wednesday. Winds directed out of the\nnorth/northeast will keep temperatures near or below average. \n\nA coastal low is expected to develop and strengthen Wednesday\njust off the northern Florida or Georgia coast before tracking\nnorth towards New England Thursday into Friday. Recent model\nguidance has trended away from a wintry precipitation scenario \non Thursday, primarily due to warm air aloft. For Thanksgiving \nDay, rain is expected to move into northern West Virginia after \n1am Thursday, gradually expanding northward through the late \nmorning hours. \n\nThere remains considerable uncertainty regarding rainfall\namounts, largely dependent on the eventual track of the surface\nlow. Current guidance continues to vary between a track inland\nacross central Pennsylvania and one that remains offshore. The\nprobability of receiving at least an inch of rainfall within a\n24-hour period (7AM Thursday to 7AM Friday) currently ranges\nfrom 30 to 40%. \n\nAs the low tracks north and winds shift to the west, colder air\nwill funnel into the region. This could support a rain-snow mix\nacross higher elevations on Friday, where accumulations could\nrange from 0.5 to 1 inch. Elsewhere, the potential for\nmeaningful winter weather impacts remains low. \n\nBrief ridging early Saturday should bring a period of drier\nweather before another shortwave approaches, bringing additional\nchances for rain or a rain-snow mix on Sunday.",
